# pockterm-sessions — session helpers for pockterm's "+" button.
#
# That first word is a marker, not decoration: deploy/install.sh copies this
# file into the projects root when nothing is there, and on the next run it has
# to tell a copy of this file — edited, as it is meant to be — from a Makefile
# that belongs to somebody else. Only the first is left alone as ours; the
# second is never written over.
#
# pockterm lists and attaches; it does not invent a way to start a session.
# When the page asks for a preset it runs `make -C $POCKTERM_SESSION_DIR
# <preset>` here, so this file — not the server — decides what a session
# actually is. The presets the page may ask for are fixed: shell, claude,
# yolo, continue (internal/session/session.go). A target missing from this
# file simply makes that button fail with make's own message.
#
# Install: deploy/install.sh does it — this file into the served account's home
# (or wherever POCKTERM_SESSION_DIR says), and the variable into the env file.
# By hand, if that root is somewhere else:
#   cp deploy/sessions.mk.example ~/work/Makefile   # edit CLAUDE below
#   POCKTERM_SESSION_DIR=~/work                     # in /etc/pockterm/pockterm.env
# Without POCKTERM_SESSION_DIR the server looks in its working directory,
# which for a systemd unit is whatever WorkingDirectory= says.

.DEFAULT_GOAL := help

# The agent command. Point it at a wrapper if sessions should be sandboxed:
# the wrapper, not this file, is then the single place that knows how.
CLAUDE ?= claude

# Where a new session starts. Override per call: make claude DIR=~/proj
DIR ?= $(CURDIR)

# What the session is named after. The page passes the folder it was started
# from (`make claude DIR=~/work/natal PREFIX=natal`), so a tab says where the
# session is rather than which command made it — claude-1, claude-2, claude-3
# is not a list anyone can navigate on a phone. Empty falls back to the
# per-target default below.
PREFIX ?=

# Which button asked for the session. Named after the folder, a tab can no
# longer say what it is — "natal" and "natal-2" are the same project started two
# different ways — so the page reads this back off the session and draws the
# button's own glyph on the tab.
#
# It is stamped here rather than recorded by the server because only this file
# knows the name: it is the one that picked the free number. Each target has its
# own default, so a session started by hand (`make yolo`) is typed as well as one
# started from a phone.
KIND ?=

.PHONY: help shell claude yolo continue custom ls kill

# spawn,<command>[,<prefix>] — start a fresh <prefix>-N session detached.
# The prefix defaults to "claude"; `shell` passes its own so the two do not
# share a numbering line.
#
# The number must be free as a session name AND as a group name: pockterm
# attaches with `new-session -t <name>`, and tmux resolves that to a group of
# that name first. A group outlives the session that formed it, so reusing the
# number would attach the phone to the old window instead of the new session.
#
# systemd-run puts the tmux server in the user's own slice. Without it a
# server first started from the web terminal inherits pockterm.service's
# cgroup, and `systemctl restart pockterm` — which any deploy does — takes
# every session down with it. The fallback keeps this working where there is
# no user manager to ask.
# The bare name is used when it is free, and only then numbered: a folder with
# one session in it is the common case, and "natal" reads better in a tab than
# "natal-1". The suffix starts at 2 for the second one, so the numbers never
# claim to be a first.
# spawn's third argument is the kind to stamp when KIND was not passed, so a
# session started from a shell here is typed like one started from the page.
# The stamp is a tmux user option on the session: it survives a rename, it is
# read back with the session list in one call, and nothing else touches it.
#
# No "=" before the name in set-option, unlike kill-session below. That prefix
# means "exact match" to the commands that take a session, and set-option reads
# its -t as a pane instead — it answers "no such session: =claude" and the stamp
# silently never lands. The plain name is safe here anyway: the session was just
# created under it, and tmux resolves an exact name before a prefix.
#
# `$$clean` is what keeps the session out of make's own business. Variables given
# on a make command line are exported to the recipe *and* travel in MAKEFLAGS, so
# without it every session started from the page carries PREFIX, DIR, KIND and CMD
# in its environment — and a `make` typed by hand inside that session inherits
# them: `make claude` there came out named after the folder of the session it was
# run from and stamped with the button that had started *that* one. MAKELEVEL goes
# too, or make reports itself as recursive.
#
# It wraps the pane's own command, and that placement is the whole point. `env -u`
# in front of `tmux` does nothing at all: the pane is started by the tmux *server*,
# which has been running since the first session and carries whatever environment
# it was started with — measured, after the obvious version of this fix changed
# nothing. tmux runs the command string through sh, so the words are split there
# and a quoted command survives unchanged.
#
# `$$hold` is why a button that cannot start says so. A command that fails on
# startup — a binary that is not installed, a typo in a custom button, `claude -c`
# in a folder with no conversation to continue ("No conversation found to
# continue", exit 1) — takes its pane with it, tmux closes the session, and on a
# phone that is a tab appearing and vanishing with nothing anywhere saying why.
# The message was on screen for the fraction of a second the session existed.
#
# So a non-zero exit drops into a shell instead, in the same directory, with the
# message still above it: the session stays until it is closed, and the pane is
# live rather than dead, so what is typed into it goes somewhere (a dead pane
# swallows keystrokes, which is the one failure this project keeps meeting).
#
# Bounded by how long the command ran, not by its status alone. `make shell` runs
# an interactive shell, and `exit` there reports the status of the last command —
# so a non-zero exit is the ordinary way out of a session somebody worked in, and
# holding that one would be a session that refuses to close. A start that fails
# fails at once: ten seconds is far above every failure above and far below any
# session anyone used.
define spawn
p="$(or $(PREFIX),$(or $(2),claude))"; \
free() { ! tmux ls 2>/dev/null | grep -qE "^$$1:|\(group $$1\)"; }; \
if free "$$p"; then s="$$p"; else n=2; while ! free "$$p-$$n"; do n=$$((n+1)); done; s="$$p-$$n"; fi; \
export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR="$${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/run/user/$$(id -u)}"; \
launch=""; \
if systemd-run --user --scope --quiet --collect true >/dev/null 2>&1; then \
	launch="systemd-run --user --scope --quiet --collect"; \
fi; \
clean="env -u PREFIX -u DIR -u KIND -u CMD -u MAKEFLAGS -u MAKELEVEL"; \
mark='t0=$$(date +%s)'; \
hold='st=$$?; [ "$$st" = 0 ] && exit 0; [ $$(( $$(date +%s) - $$t0 )) -ge 10 ] && exit "$$st"; printf "\n\033[1;31m[exited %s — the session is held so this can be read]\033[0m\n" "$$st"; exec $${SHELL:-/bin/sh}'; \
cmd=$(1); \
$$launch tmux new-session -d -s "$$s" -c "$(DIR)" "$$mark; $$clean $$cmd; $$hold"; \
k="$(or $(KIND),$(3))"; \
if [ -n "$$k" ]; then tmux set-option -t "$$s" @pockterm-kind "$$k"; fi; \
echo "started $$s in $(DIR)$${launch:+ (own slice)}"
endef

help: ## Show this help
	@grep -hE '^[a-z][a-z-]*:.*## ' $(MAKEFILE_LIST) | \
		awk 'BEGIN {FS = ":.*## "}; {printf "  make %-9s %s\n", $$1, $$2}'

shell: ## New plain shell session (its own numbering line)
	@$(call spawn,$${SHELL:-/bin/sh},shell,shell)

claude: ## New agent session
	@$(call spawn,$(CLAUDE),,claude)

yolo: ## New agent session that does not ask for permissions
	@$(call spawn,'$(CLAUDE) --dangerously-skip-permissions',,yolo)

continue: ## New agent session continuing the last conversation
	@$(call spawn,'$(CLAUDE) -c',,continue)

# The buttons the owner adds in the drawer's settings all come here, with their
# command in CMD. One target rather than one per agent: adding `qwen` on a phone
# must not mean editing this file, which on a real host is written by ansible.
#
# What may be in CMD is decided before it gets here — letters, digits, spaces and
# - _ . / = : , @ + and nothing that could end the quoting below or start an
# expansion (internal/session/custom.go). Keep the quotes: the command is one
# argument to the launcher, not a line of shell.
#
# Sandboxing belongs to $(AGENT) for the same reason it belongs to $(CLAUDE):
# point it at a wrapper and every button inherits it, unchanged.
#
# It has to be a wrapper that runs its arguments (`nice`, `sg <group> -c`,
# `systemd-run --scope`, …). One that supplies its own command and passes the
# arguments to *that* — which is what an agent launcher usually does — would
# start the wrong thing and look like it worked: the author's own role sets
# `AGENT_COMMAND=<first word of CMD>` on such a launcher instead of prepending it.
AGENT ?=

custom: ## New session running CMD (the drawer's custom buttons)
	@if [ -z "$(CMD)" ]; then echo "usage: make custom CMD='qwen'"; exit 2; fi
	@$(call spawn,'$(AGENT) $(CMD)',custom,custom)

ls: ## List sessions, hiding the clients pockterm attaches with
	@tmux ls 2>/dev/null | grep -v '^pockterm-client-' || echo "no sessions"

# close,<session> — close a session and the client sessions holding its windows.
#
# The second half is not tidiness. pockterm attaches with `new-session -t <name>`,
# so while any page has that session open there is a second member of its group,
# and `kill-session` closes one session only: the window another member still
# holds stays open with its process running. Closing from here would then leave
# exactly what closing from the phone left before 2026-08-27 — the session gone,
# the agent working on, and nothing in `make ls` saying so, client sessions being
# hidden from it. The server does the same thing in internal/session/close.go,
# where the measurement is written down.
#
# The group's name, not the session's: tmux names a group after the session it
# was created from and never renames it, so a renamed session no longer matches
# the group its clients are in. A session nobody has open has no group at all,
# and then this is the one kill it always was.
#
# The clients go first. Killing the session first leaves the window standing on a
# client alone, and a page attached to that draws a live tab for a session tmux
# has already forgotten.
#
# Only sessions this server names for itself are touched. Two sessions of the
# owner's own cannot share a group through pockterm, and if a hand-made pair ever
# did, closing one must not take the other's windows with it.
define close
n="$(1)"; \
grp=$$(tmux ls -F '#{session_name} #{session_group}' 2>/dev/null | awk -v n="$$n" '$$1==n {print $$2}'); \
if [ -n "$$grp" ]; then \
	tmux ls -F '#{session_name} #{session_group}' 2>/dev/null \
		| awk -v g="$$grp" '$$2==g && $$1 ~ /^pockterm-client-/ {print $$1}' \
		| while read -r c; do tmux kill-session -t "=$$c" 2>/dev/null || true; done; \
fi; \
tmux kill-session -t "=$$n" 2>/dev/null
endef

kill: ## Kill one session: make kill NAME=claude-2
	@if [ -z "$(NAME)" ]; then echo "usage: make kill NAME=<session>  (see: make ls)"; \
	 elif $(call close,$(NAME)); then echo "killed $(NAME)"; \
	 else echo "no session $(NAME)"; fi
